// A SERVING ARM IS A CHECKPOINT, A RUNTIME AND A TOPOLOGY -- NOT A MODEL.
//
// WHY THIS AXIS IS BEING ADDED, AND WHAT IT COST TO DISCOVER. gunbc.harness.harness_reasoning_wire
// carries a serving-unit coproduct whose GLM arm is a single `Glm53FlashOnVllm`. That collapse was
// defensible when only one GLM realization was contemplated, and it broke the moment a second one
// existed. "GLM on vLLM" is not one unit: measured on 2026-09-08, the fleet's pinned DeepSeek-era
// image does not register `Glm5NextForConditionalGeneration` AT ALL, while a community SM121 image
// registers it, ships a GLM reasoning parser, and returns reasoning under a DIFFERENT MEMBER NAME
// than the model authority declares. Same model, same wire protocol family, incompatible units.
//
// THE AXES ARE INDEPENDENT AND EACH ONE DECIDES SOMETHING THE OTHERS CANNOT. A checkpoint fixes the
// loader, the quantization, the bytes and the footprint; a runtime fixes the architecture registry,
// the parsers, the attention backend and the response schema; a topology fixes the aggregate memory
// and therefore which checkpoints are admissible at all. A model identity determines none of them,
// which is precisely why one `Glm53FlashOnVllm` arm could not answer for two live realizations.

type ServingCheckpoint
  = Glm53NativeFp8
  | Glm53Exl3_205
  | Glm53Nvfp4CompressedTensors
  | DeepseekV4FlashIq3sSplit

type ServingRuntime
  = GunbcSparkrunVllmDs4Gb10
  | Gitcommit90Sm121Exl3
  | Tonyd2wildSm121Nvfp4
  | VllmOpenaiGlm53Flash
  | GunbcGlm53NopeDerivedGb10

type ServingTopology
  = SingleSparkTp1
  | SparkPairTp2
  | FourSparkTp4

fn serving_topology_rank_count(t: ServingTopology) -> Nat {
  match t {
    SingleSparkTp1 => 1
    SparkPairTp2 => 2
    FourSparkTp4 => 4
  }
}

// ── THE RANK COUNT BACK TO A TOPOLOGY, DERIVED FROM THE FORWARD MAP AND TOTAL ─────────────────
//
// WHY THIS IS A SEARCH OVER THE ROSTER AND NOT A SECOND TABLE. `serving_topology_rank_count` above
// already owns the degree-to-topology relation; hand-spelling its inverse would be that one fact
// written twice, and the two copies would drift the moment a topology is added. So the inverse is
// computed FROM the forward function, and the only thing this declares is which topologies exist.
//
// AND IT IS OPTIONAL BECAUSE IT IS PARTIAL. A rank count no topology carries has no answer, and the
// caller is the one that must say what to do about it.
data serving_topologies: List<ServingTopology> = [
  SingleSparkTp1,
  SparkPairTp2,
  FourSparkTp4,
]

fn serving_topology_of_rank_count(ranks: Nat) -> ServingTopology? {
  first(filter(serving_topologies, t => serving_topology_rank_count(t: t) == ranks))
}

// ── THE CANONICAL PROJECTION OF EACH AXIS, WHICH IS WHERE EQUALITY IS DECIDED ─────────────────
//
// One place knows the variants and the wire is that place. A consumer asking whether two serving
// measurements describe one configuration must not spell the comparison as a per-variant match of
// its own: a second match over these variants is a second authority on what distinguishes them, and
// it goes stale silently the moment a checkpoint or runtime is added here.
// gunbc.spark.serving_performance_subject is that consumer.
fn serving_checkpoint_wire(c: ServingCheckpoint) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match c {
    Glm53NativeFp8 => "glm-5.3-flash-native-fp8" as NonEmptyStr
    Glm53Exl3_205 => "glm-5.3-flash-exl3-2.05" as NonEmptyStr
    Glm53Nvfp4CompressedTensors => "glm-5.3-flash-nvfp4-compressed-tensors" as NonEmptyStr
    DeepseekV4FlashIq3sSplit => "deepseek-v4-flash-iq3s-split" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// THE TWO ROWS THIS BRANCH ADDS GET WIRES HERE RATHER THAN ANYWHERE ELSE, and the exhaustive match
// is what forced the question: adding a ServingRuntime variant without a wire does not compile, so
// a runtime cannot reach the subject authority unnamed.
fn serving_runtime_wire(r: ServingRuntime) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match r {
    GunbcSparkrunVllmDs4Gb10 => "gunbc-sparkrun-vllm-ds4-gb10" as NonEmptyStr
    Gitcommit90Sm121Exl3 => "gitcommit90-sm121-exl3" as NonEmptyStr
    Tonyd2wildSm121Nvfp4 => "tonyd2wild-sm121-nvfp4" as NonEmptyStr
    VllmOpenaiGlm53Flash => "vllm-openai-glm53-flash" as NonEmptyStr
    GunbcGlm53NopeDerivedGb10 => "gunbc-glm53-nope-derived-gb10" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn serving_topology_wire(t: ServingTopology) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match t {
    SingleSparkTp1 => "tp1" as NonEmptyStr
    SparkPairTp2 => "tp2" as NonEmptyStr
    FourSparkTp4 => "tp4" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// ONE GB10's UNIFIED POOL, read off a live member on 2026-09-08 (`free -g` on spark-0c75 reported
// 121 GiB total). It is carried as a per-unit figure because aggregate admissibility is the product
// of this and the rank count, and stating the aggregate directly would hide which half moved.
data gb10_unified_pool_gib: Nat = 121

fn serving_topology_aggregate_pool_gib(t: ServingTopology) -> Nat {
  serving_topology_rank_count(t: t) * gb10_unified_pool_gib
}

// ── CHECKPOINT FACTS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THESE ARE THE FACTS THAT DECIDE FIT, and they belong to the checkpoint rather than to the model.
// The same GLM-5.3-Flash is 306 GiB of native FP8 weights and 79.3 GiB of EXL3 2.05bpw; a topology
// that admits one refuses the other. A model-identity row cannot express that difference, which is
// how a branch came to pin a checkpoint that could not fit the group it targeted.
// PLACEMENT TRAVELS WITH THE CHECKPOINT ROW, so the one value that decides install path, download,
// verification, mount and launch path is declared once beside the identity it belongs to. A
// checkpoint's bytes are somewhere specific on a host; pretending every checkpoint lives in the same
// layout is what made a modelled run report an ABSENT snapshot while 80 GiB of exactly that revision
// sat on the same disk serving a live engine.
//
// REVIEW PROPOSED MOVING IT ONTO THE ARM'S SNAPSHOT BINDING, AND IT IS DECLINED HERE WITH A REASON
// RATHER THAN APPLIED OR IGNORED. The proposal's premise is sound -- where bytes land is a deployment
// choice, and deployment choices are the arm's. What it does not price is that keying placement by ARM
// makes a specific bad state WRITABLE: two arms naming one checkpoint could place it differently, and
// nothing in the type would refuse them.
//
// THAT STATE IS NOT MERELY UNTIDY, IT BREAKS A MECHANISM THAT DEPENDS ON ITS ABSENCE. Rail replication
// (gunbc.spark.model_snapshot_acquisition model_snapshot_rail_source) fetches a snapshot from a peer
// into the SAME path it resolves locally, and it is entitled to do that only because placement is a
// function of the checkpoint and not of the host or the arm. Arm-keyed placement would make peer and
// local paths diverge for one revision, which rsync cannot detect -- it would copy into a directory
// that is correct for neither arm. The fleet would also hold two copies of eighty gigabytes with no
// row saying it meant to.
//
// SO THE CURRENT SHAPE IS THE CONSTRUCTION AND THE PROPOSAL IS THE VALIDATION, which is the ordering
// DESIGN section 5 asks for: keyed by checkpoint, divergent placement for one revision has no
// spelling; keyed by arm, it is writable and would need a check to catch it afterwards. If a future
// arm genuinely needs one checkpoint in two locations -- a staging copy beside a serving copy -- that
// is a second checkpoint row with its own identity and provenance, not a placement field that has
// quietly stopped being a function of the thing it names.
// ── WHO SPELLS THE QUANTISATION: THE LAUNCH, OR THE CHECKPOINT ────────────────────────────────
//
// THIS WAS A `NonEmptyStr` BESIDE A BOOL ABOUT SOMETHING ELSE, and the two got joined by accident. The
// launch renderer decided whether to pass `--quantization` by reading
// `requires_upstream_glm53_integration` -- a fact about what the RUNTIME must support -- because the native
// checkpoint happened to be the only row where both answers coincided. Two independent facts sharing one
// carrier means the first row where they disagree silently renders the wrong argv.
//
// SO THE SCHEME AND ITS DECLARER ARE ONE VALUE. `exl3` must be spelled, because nothing in that checkpoint's
// config tells the engine to load an EXL3 kernel; `fp8` must NOT be, because the checkpoint's config already
// carries the scheme together with the block size and exclusion list that a bare flag would discard. That is
// not a preference about verbosity: it changes which weights the engine builds.
//
// AND IT IS WHAT THE CONVERGENCE ASSESSOR NEEDS. An observed line missing `--quantization` used to read as
// an UNKNOWN, which made the native deployment permanently unrecognisable -- the arm could never converge
// because its own correct argv looked incomplete. With the declarer modelled, "the line spells no scheme" is
// a state the desired identity can also be in, and the two match.
type CheckpointQuantization
  = QuantizationSpelledOnArgv { wire: NonEmptyStr }
  | QuantizationResolvedFromCheckpointConfig { wire: NonEmptyStr, because: NonEmptyStr }

// PRESENT ONLY WHERE THE LAUNCH ITSELF SAYS IT. This is the one projection both the renderer and the
// convergence identity read, so they cannot disagree about whether a flag is on the line.
fn checkpoint_quantization_on_argv(q: CheckpointQuantization) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match q {
    QuantizationSpelledOnArgv { wire: w } => Present { value: w }
    QuantizationResolvedFromCheckpointConfig { wire: _, because: _ } => none
  }
}

type ServingCheckpointRow {
  checkpoint: ServingCheckpoint
  repo: HubRepoId
  revision: NonEmptyStr
  placement: SnapshotPlacement
  architecture: NonEmptyStr
  weight_gib: Gibibyte
  quantization: CheckpointQuantization
  provenance: NonEmptyStr
}

data glm53_exl3_205_row: ServingCheckpointRow = ServingCheckpointRow {
  checkpoint: Glm53Exl3_205,
  repo: HubRepoId { kind: HubModelRepo, path: "turboderp/GLM-5.3-Flash-exl3" as NonEmptyStr },
  revision: "51058cd551c7e570d87bd32a4adee720edce2349" as NonEmptyStr,
  placement: LocalDirectoryPlacement { host_path: "/home/briansrls/models/glm53-flash-exl3-2.05" as NonEmptyStr },
  architecture: "Glm5NextForConditionalGeneration" as NonEmptyStr,
  weight_gib: gibibyte(count: 80),
  quantization: QuantizationSpelledOnArgv { wire: "exl3" as NonEmptyStr },
  provenance: "revision resolved and fetched 2026-09-08: the Hub model API reported 24 files totalling 85.23 GB for this revision, and the engine read the materialised tree back as 79.30 GiB across 12 safetensors shards; the architecture is the sole entry of `architectures` in config.json fetched at this exact revision on 2026-09-11" as NonEmptyStr,
}

data glm53_native_fp8_row: ServingCheckpointRow = ServingCheckpointRow {
  checkpoint: Glm53NativeFp8,
  repo: HubRepoId { kind: HubModelRepo, path: "zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash" as NonEmptyStr },
  revision: glm_5_3_flash_native_manifest.revision,
  placement: HubCachePlacement { cache_root: spark_hub_cache_root },
  architecture: "Glm5NextForConditionalGeneration" as NonEmptyStr,
  weight_gib: glm_manifest_gibibytes(m: glm_5_3_flash_native_manifest),
  quantization: QuantizationResolvedFromCheckpointConfig { wire: "fp8" as NonEmptyStr, because: "the checkpoint's own config.json carries quant_method fp8 together with its weight_block_size and a 1509-entry modules_to_not_convert list; passing --quantization fp8 overrides that structure with a bare scheme name, so the launch says nothing and the engine reads the checkpoint" as NonEmptyStr },
  provenance: "revision and byte population are extdeps.zhipu.glm_5_3_flash glm_5_3_flash_native_manifest, read from the Hub model API at that exact revision. The GiB figure is DERIVED from those bytes rather than authored: this row previously carried a hand-written 306, which agreed with two other spellings of the same fact and could therefore never catch their first divergence -- the merge that brought the architecture field in is exactly where that divergence would have arrived unnoticed. Still NOT measured on this fleet -- nothing here has fetched it. The architecture is the sole entry of `architectures` in config.json fetched at this exact revision on 2026-09-11" as NonEmptyStr,
}

fn serving_checkpoint_row(c: ServingCheckpoint) -> ServingCheckpointRow? {
  match c {
    Glm53Exl3_205 => Present { value: glm53_exl3_205_row }
    Glm53NativeFp8 => Present { value: glm53_native_fp8_row }
    Glm53Nvfp4CompressedTensors => none
    DeepseekV4FlashIq3sSplit => none
  }
}

// ── RUNTIME FACTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// WHETHER A RUNTIME CAN LOAD AN ARCHITECTURE IS A MEASURED PROPERTY OF THE IMAGE, and it is the fact
// that stopped this migration. It is carried per runtime, established by executing the registry
// probe inside the image, and never inferred from the model card or the upstream recipe -- both of
// which describe a vLLM newer than the one a given image pins.
// THE IMAGE, ITS PINNED DIGEST AND THE VERSION IT DECLARES ARE READ FROM THE IMAGE'S OWN MODULE, not
// restated here. Both rows previously carried a literal digest and a literal version beside the
// extdeps module that owned them, which is two independently editable copies of one pin: a re-pin in
// extdeps would have left this row launching the old digest with nothing refusing. The row keeps the
// fields because a serving arm genuinely has an image and a version; what it no longer has is a
// second place they are written.
//
// THE HUB CLI'S PATH IS A PER-IMAGE FACT, measured inside each image rather than assumed common.
// `/opt/env/bin/hf` in the DeepSeek runtime and `/usr/local/bin/hf` in the community one -- a single
// hardcoded path silently produced a container that could not start, reported downstream as a
// checkpoint whose bytes were wrong.
// THE IMAGE CLAIMS A SOURCE REVISION AND THE SELF-REPORTED VERSION IS NOT IT. `vllm_version` is what
// the running process says about itself, and extdeps.vllm.server refuses that as provenance for
// exactly the right reason: a version prefix is a lexical fact about a response body. Deriving the
// revision whose DEFAULTS govern this image by parsing that string would be the authority substitution
// extdeps.vllm.runtime_defaults was written to remove, committed in the module that consumes it.
//
// SO THE CLAIM IS ITS OWN FIELD, and what it authorises is narrow. `claimed_defaults_revision` names
// the revision this image is BUILT FROM according to its publisher, which is enough to say what an
// omitted flag resolves to for a launch that has already happened and been observed. It is NOT
// evidence that the image contains that revision's bytes: nothing here has compared them, and
// extdeps.vllm.runtime_defaults keeps that question in its own binding type rather than folding it
// into the value.
// -- WHETHER AN ENGINE CAN LOAD THIS CHECKPOINT'S TENSOR LAYOUT --------------------------------
//
// THE WALL THIS FLEET LEARNED BY LOSING A SERVICE, AND IT IS NOT A ROW FIELD. `registers_glm5_next` answers
// whether the architecture is in the registry and the quantisation roster answers whether fp8 is
// implemented; BOTH were true of the image that failed on 2026-09-10, which loaded the native FP8 checkpoint
// far enough to form a four-rank NCCL group and then died with
// KeyError 'layers.0.self_attn.k_proj.weight'. The loader expected a plain k_proj that this architecture does
// not have -- it uses fused_qkvbfg_a_proj and conv1d projections, with 1509 modules held out of quantisation
// -- so the missing fact was the WEIGHT MAPPING, which neither per-row wall asks about.
//
// AND IT CANNOT BE ASKED PER ROW, WHICH IS WHY THE ATTEMPT TO IS GONE. "Does this runtime carry the GLM-5.3
// integration" has no answer independent of the checkpoint being loaded and the kernel path it takes: the
// official image carries the loader and refuses the NoPE MLA kernel, the community image serves NoPE MLA and
// cannot map the weights. A Bool on a checkpoint row joined to a standing on a runtime row could express
// neither. gunbc.spark.serving_compatibility asks it at the grain that has an answer.
// THE PER-IMAGE NATIVE STANDING WAS DELETED HERE, BECAUSE IT WAS THE SAME FACT TWICE.
//
// This row used to carry `native_runtime_standing: NativeRuntimeStanding` -- three arms saying whether an
// IMAGE carried the GLM-5.3 loader integration -- and gunbc.spark.serving_compatibility was then added
// carrying the same question as a RELATION over checkpoint x runtime x topology x accelerator x profile. Both
// authorities survived the same commit, which is the fork DESIGN section 3 exists to prevent: an image is
// refuted FOR A CHECKPOINT, and the standing stored on the row could not say which, so it answered for every
// checkpoint at once. That is exactly why the EXL3 arm needed a sentence inside its own refusal cause
// explaining that the refutation was "of this checkpoint on this image, not of the image".
//
// EVERY FACT THOSE ARMS CARRIED SURVIVES AS A MEASURED STANDING. The gitcommit90 weight-mapping failure is
// glm_native_on_gitcommit90_standing; the official image's kernel refusal is
// glm_native_official_sm121_tp4_standing; the EXL3 success is glm_exl3_on_gitcommit90_standing. Nothing was
// dropped -- the same receipts now sit at the grain that can name their subject.
//
// AND THE INTENT SPLIT MOVED WITH THEM. StaticIntegrationObserved existed so that an inspected-but-unrun
// image could carry a bounded experiment and not normal serving. That asymmetry now lives in the
// LAUNCH PLANNER, which reads the measured roster's admission for the exact subject and refuses an
// intent of normal serving when the roster admits only an experiment -- gunbc.spark.serving_arm_launch
// intent_refusal, over gunbc.spark.serving_compatibility measured_admission_for.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS PARAGRAPH CLAIMED A DIFFERENT HOME and was wrong in the way DESIGN 4c
// warns about: it said the asymmetry was carried by a selection fold, while the admission vocabulary
// sat imported-and-uncalled and no selection module read these standings at all. The wall was deleted
// and its stated replacement did not exist, so the prose was the only thing still asserting it.
// WHAT AN ARM IS BEING ASKED FOR, because the same tuple is admissible for one and not the other. An
// experiment may run on a statically-inspected image precisely so that the feasibility receipt can
// exist; ordinary serving may not, because a bounded trial and a service carry different consequences
// for anyone depending on the endpoint.
type ArmIntent
  = ArmBoundedExperiment
  | ArmNormalServing

// ── A RUNTIME IS IDENTIFIED BY ITS BYTES AND ITS LINEAGE, NOT BY A STRING ──────────────────────
//
// `image_reference: NonEmptyStr` WAS DOING THREE JOBS AT ONCE. It was the thing docker pulls, the thing a
// compatibility subject is keyed by, and the only record of where the image came from -- and one of the three
// rows in this module does not even carry a digest: the DeepSeek image reads
// "aidendle94/sparkrun-vllm-ds4-gb10 (digest pinned in extdeps.docker.images.sparkrun_vllm_ds4_gb10)", a
// sentence about a pin rather than a pin. Comparing that as if it were bytes is the same defect a review just
// found one level up, where a subject was keyed by an enum variant name.
//
// SO THE PIN IS ITS OWN QUESTION AND HAS TWO HONEST ANSWERS. A digest-pinned reference IS the bytes and can
// be compared as an identity; a reference pinned somewhere else names its authority and must not be treated
// as one, because a tag is mutable and two pulls of it are not the same artifact.
type RuntimeArtifactPin
  = PinnedByDigest { reference: NonEmptyStr }
  | PinnedElsewhere { reference: NonEmptyStr, pin_authority: NonEmptyStr }

fn runtime_pin_reference(p: RuntimeArtifactPin) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match p {
    PinnedByDigest { reference: r } => r
    PinnedElsewhere { reference: r, pin_authority: _ } => r
  }
}

// AND THE BYTES-AS-IDENTITY PROJECTION IS AN OPTIONAL, so a caller keying a subject on it cannot silently
// accept a mutable tag. This is the fail-closed half: an unpinned runtime has no identity to compare, and
// saying so is different from saying the tag is one.
fn runtime_pin_as_identity(p: RuntimeArtifactPin) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match p {
    PinnedByDigest { reference: r } => Present { value: r }
    PinnedElsewhere { reference: _, pin_authority: _ } => none
  }
}

// THE LINEAGE IS MODELLED BECAUSE THE NEXT RUNTIME THIS FLEET NEEDS IS A DERIVATIVE. The repair for the NoPE
// MLA refusal is an image built from a named upstream vLLM revision with two commits backported and a
// FlashInfer revision pinned -- and a derived image whose row recorded only a digest would be
// indistinguishable from an official one, so nothing could say WHICH patch set a receipt was measured
// against. `patch_set` is empty for an image taken as published, which is a fact rather than an absence.
type RuntimeArtifactIdentity sole_constructor {
  pin: RuntimeArtifactPin
  source_revision: NonEmptyStr
  patch_set: List<NonEmptyStr>
  provenance: NonEmptyStr
}

fn runtime_artifact_identity(
  pin: RuntimeArtifactPin,
  source_revision: NonEmptyStr,
  patch_set: List<NonEmptyStr>,
  provenance: NonEmptyStr,
) -> RuntimeArtifactIdentity {
  RuntimeArtifactIdentity {
    pin: pin, source_revision: source_revision, patch_set: patch_set, provenance: provenance,
  }
}

// WHAT DOCKER IS GIVEN, which is a different question from what a subject is keyed by: a launch can pull an
// unpinned tag, and a receipt must not claim that pull was an identity.
fn runtime_artifact_wire(a: RuntimeArtifactIdentity) -> NonEmptyStr {
  runtime_pin_reference(p: a.pin)
}

// WHAT A SUBJECT IS KEYED BY. The patch set is part of it: the same base revision with and without the NoPE
// writer backport are different artifacts, and a key that dropped the patch set would let a measurement taken
// on stock bytes refuse the patched image -- which is the entire repair.
fn runtime_artifact_key(a: RuntimeArtifactIdentity) -> String {
  match runtime_pin_as_identity(p: a.pin) {
    Absent => join(["<unpinned:", runtime_pin_reference(p: a.pin) as String, ">"], "")
    Present { value: digest } =>
      join([
        digest as String, " src=", a.source_revision as String,
        " patches=", if count(a.patch_set) == 0 { "none" } else { join(a.patch_set, ",") },
      ], "")
  }
}

// AN IMAGE NOBODY HAS PROBED HAS NO READING, AND THAT IS NOT AN EMPTY REGISTRY. `registry_reading`
// arrived from the other side of a merge as a REQUIRED record, which is right for every row that side
// carried -- each was probed inside its own digest. This branch adds a row for the STOCK upstream image,
// whose registry has not been read by anyone here, and the three ways to make an unprobed image fit a
// required shape are all lies: an empty architecture list asserts a measured negative, a copied list
// asserts a reading taken somewhere else, and a reference that deliberately mismatches games the
// currency check into refusing for the wrong reason. Absent is the fourth option and the only true one,
// and it is fail-closed at both readers: an unprobed image registers nothing and its reading is never
// current.
// HOW REASONING COMES BACK IS OBSERVED FROM A LAUNCHED ENGINE, SO AN UNLAUNCHED RUNTIME HAS NO ANSWER.
// This field was required, and a required field forces every new row to assert something: the model
// authority's member name (which this file already records as WRONG for the community image -- it returns
// `reasoning`, not `reasoning_content`), or a neighbour's observed value, or a guess dressed as a
// default. Those are the same three lies the registry_reading annotation below enumerates, and the same
// fourth option is the only true one.
//
// IT IS THEREFORE OPTIONAL, AND ABSENT IS FAIL-CLOSED AT ITS READERS. A runtime that has never served
// cannot say how its reasoning comes back, and must not be made to. The ordering this admits is the one
// the fleet actually has: a row exists so a launch can be PLANNED -- the launch argv reads only the
// artifact and the defaults revision, never this field -- and the projection is filled in from the
// completion that launch produces.
// THE QUANTISATION FLAG IS PASSED ONLY WHERE IT IS THE REQUEST, AND FOR THE NATIVE CHECKPOINT IT IS NOT.
// A previous revision passed `--quantization fp8` for every arm so the running line would describe its
// own artifact to the convergence assessor, and defended it at length. That was wrong twice over: the
// engine reads quant_method from the checkpoint's own config either way, so the flag adds nothing for
// this arm, and overriding it is how a launch discards the checkpoint's weight_block_size and its
// 1509-entry modules_to_not_convert list. The identity the assessor needs comes from the REVISION PATH,
// which every line carries and which determines the weights uniquely.
//
// AND THE CONDITION IS NOW THE FACT ITSELF. It used to read `requires_upstream_glm53_integration` -- a fact
// about what the RUNTIME must support -- which gave the right answer only because the native checkpoint was
// the single row where the two questions coincided.
fn checkpoint_quantization_argv(row: ServingCheckpointRow) -> List<String> {
  match checkpoint_quantization_on_argv(q: row.quantization) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: w } => ["--quantization", w as String]
  }
}

type ServingRuntimeRow {
  runtime: ServingRuntime
  artifact: RuntimeArtifactIdentity
  hub_cli_path: NonEmptyStr
  vllm_version: NonEmptyStr
  registry_reading: ArchitectureRegistryReading?
  reasoning_projection: ReasoningResponseProjection?
  provenance: NonEmptyStr
  claimed_defaults_revision: ModelledVllmDefaultsRevision?
}

// HOW REASONING COMES BACK IS A RUNTIME PROPERTY WITH TWO SHAPES, AND THE MEMBER NAME IS PART OF IT.
// An earlier revision of the harness seam asked only WHETHER the server parses reasoning, and took
// the member NAME from the model authority (`reasoning_content`, DeepSeek's guide) with `reasoning`
// demoted to an alias. Measured against the community SM121 image on 2026-09-08, the response
// carries `reasoning` and has NO `reasoning_content` member at all -- so the model authority's name
// is not this unit's primary, and the alias fold rescued the read by accident of ordering rather
// than by the model being right.
type ReasoningResponseProjection
  = ParsedReasoningField { field: NonEmptyStr }
  | InlineReasoning { end_token: NonEmptyStr }

data gitcommit90_sm121_exl3_row: ServingRuntimeRow = ServingRuntimeRow {
  runtime: Gitcommit90Sm121Exl3,
  artifact: runtime_artifact_identity(
    pin: PinnedByDigest { reference: gitcommit90_glm_53_one_spark_pinned_reference() },
    source_revision: (defaults_source_revision(r: Vllm487ecf187)).full_revision,
    patch_set: [],
    provenance: "a community image taken as published; the revision is what it self-reports as its vLLM build, not a comparison of its bytes against that tree" as NonEmptyStr,
  ),
  hub_cli_path: "/usr/local/bin/hf" as NonEmptyStr,
  vllm_version: gitcommit90_glm_53_one_spark_vllm_version,
  registry_reading: Present { value: gitcommit90_glm_53_one_spark_registry_reading },
  reasoning_projection: Present { value: ParsedReasoningField { field: "reasoning" as NonEmptyStr } },
  provenance: "registry probe executed inside this exact digest on 2026-09-08 returned SUPPORTED: True for Glm5NextForConditionalGeneration alongside Glm5NextForCausalLM and Glm5NextMTPModel; a completion against the launched engine returned message members including `reasoning` and no `reasoning_content`. The full registry carried here was re-read inside the same digest on spark-a3ee (compute capability 12.1) on 2026-09-11: 377 entries, the three Glm5Next entries among them and DeepseekV41ForCausalLM not" as NonEmptyStr,
  claimed_defaults_revision: Present { value: Vllm487ecf187 },
}

data gunbc_sparkrun_ds4_row: ServingRuntimeRow = ServingRuntimeRow {
  runtime: GunbcSparkrunVllmDs4Gb10,
  artifact: runtime_artifact_identity(
    pin: PinnedByDigest { reference: sparkrun_vllm_ds4_gb10_pinned_reference() },
    source_revision: (defaults_source_revision(r: Vllm1967a5627)).full_revision,
    patch_set: [],
    provenance: "the digest is READ FROM its authority rather than restated: extdeps.docker.images.sparkrun_vllm_ds4_gb10 sparkrun_vllm_ds4_gb10_pinned_reference builds repository@sha256:digest from the rows that module owns. Two earlier shapes were both wrong in opposite directions -- one carried the SENTENCE \"digest pinned in extdeps.docker.images...\" inside the reference string, which read as a pin and was prose about one; the correction for it answered PinnedElsewhere and dropped the digest out of the VALUE, which left runtime_artifact_wire handing docker a bare repository name that resolves to :latest. Naming an authority and calling it are different acts, and only the second puts the bytes in the launch" as NonEmptyStr,
  ),
  hub_cli_path: "/opt/env/bin/hf" as NonEmptyStr,
  vllm_version: sparkrun_vllm_ds4_gb10_vllm_version,
  registry_reading: Present { value: sparkrun_vllm_ds4_gb10_registry_reading },
  reasoning_projection: Present { value: InlineReasoning { end_token: "</think>" as NonEmptyStr } },
  provenance: "registry probe executed inside the running container on the group A head 2026-09-08 returned SUPPORTED: False for Glm5NextForConditionalGeneration, with thirteen GLM architectures present and none of them Glm5; vllm/reasoning/ holds twenty parser modules and none is GLM, while vllm/tool_parsers/glm47_moe_tool_parser.py IS present. The full registry carried here was read inside the pinned digest on spark-a3ee (compute capability 12.1) on 2026-09-11: 361 entries, no Glm5 entry and no DeepseekV41ForCausalLM" as NonEmptyStr,
  claimed_defaults_revision: Present { value: Vllm1967a5627 },
}

// THE IMAGE UPSTREAM PRESCRIBES FOR THIS MODEL, PINNED TO WHAT THE FLEET ACTUALLY PULLED. It is its
// own runtime rather than a relabelling of the EXL3 one, because it is a different artifact with a
// different loader, and reusing that variant would have made the refuted row and the candidate row the
// same row.
//
// ITS STANDING IS STATIC, NOT FEASIBLE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE SPLIT. What has been
// established by inspection on this hardware: the digest resolves identically on the group's hosts, the
// platform is linux/arm64, the entrypoint is ["vllm","serve"], all three Glm5Next architectures are
// registered, the multi-node mp fields (nnodes, node_rank, master_addr, master_port) are present,
// FlashInfer is 0.6.18 -- which clears the 0.6.18 sparse-MLA floor and not merely the 0.6.17
// prerequisite -- and a bf16 matmul EXECUTES on the GB10 at compute capability 12.1 despite
// TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST stopping at 12.0, which is CUDA 13 family compatibility rather than an
// assumption.
//
// WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED IS THAT IT LOADS THIS CHECKPOINT. The previous attempt proved how far an
// image can get while being unable to: architecture registered, fp8 implemented, four ranks joined, and
// then a KeyError in the weight loader. So this row may authorise a bounded experiment and may not
// support serving until a run says otherwise.
data vllm_openai_glm53_flash_row: ServingRuntimeRow = ServingRuntimeRow {
  runtime: VllmOpenaiGlm53Flash,
  artifact: runtime_artifact_identity(
    pin: PinnedByDigest { reference: "vllm/vllm-openai@sha256:819ec9c063412e5730d1b0e82046ba540d1bf991f3c4f661a849aae8a0c52374" as NonEmptyStr },
    source_revision: glm_5_3_flash_vllm_integration_commit,
    patch_set: [],
    provenance: "the image upstream prescribes for this model, pinned to the digest the group's hosts actually resolved on 2026-09-10. NO patches: this is the stock artifact, which is why its NoPE MLA refusal is a fact about upstream rather than about a local build" as NonEmptyStr,
  ),
  hub_cli_path: "/usr/local/bin/hf" as NonEmptyStr,
  vllm_version: "0.28.1rc1.dev580+g385dce36b" as NonEmptyStr,
  registry_reading: none,
  reasoning_projection: none,
  provenance: "pulled by tag vllm/vllm-openai:glm53-flash on the group B hosts 2026-09-10 and pinned here to the digest that tag resolved to; recipes.vllm.ai/zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash directs NVIDIA deployments to this image for this checkpoint" as NonEmptyStr,
  claimed_defaults_revision: none,
}

fn serving_runtime_row(r: ServingRuntime) -> ServingRuntimeRow? {
  match r {
    Gitcommit90Sm121Exl3 => Present { value: gitcommit90_sm121_exl3_row }
    GunbcSparkrunVllmDs4Gb10 => Present { value: gunbc_sparkrun_ds4_row }
    Tonyd2wildSm121Nvfp4 => none
    VllmOpenaiGlm53Flash => Present { value: vllm_openai_glm53_flash_row }
    GunbcGlm53NopeDerivedGb10 => Present { value: glm53_nope_derived_row }
  }
}

// ── ADMISSION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A TUPLE IS ADMITTED OR REFUSED, AND THE REFUSAL NAMES WHICH AXIS FAILED. Two independent walls,
// both measured rather than assumed, and either alone is disqualifying -- which is exactly the state
// the branch was in when it pinned native FP8 against the DeepSeek image.
type ServingArmVerdict
  = ServingArmAdmitted
  | ServingArmRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }



// THE FOOTPRINT ALLOWANCE IS DELIBERATELY NOT A UTILISATION FRACTION. Weights are not the whole
// requirement: the engine also needs KV, activations and graph memory, and the recipe's own runtime
// minimum for native FP8 exceeds its weight size by a quarter. A checkpoint is admitted only when
// the aggregate pool exceeds the weights with headroom, and the headroom is stated rather than
// tuned away.
data serving_arm_headroom_numerator: Nat = 5
data serving_arm_headroom_denominator: Nat = 4

fn serving_arm_fits(c: ServingCheckpointRow, t: ServingTopology) -> Bool {
  serving_topology_aggregate_pool_gib(t: t) * serving_arm_headroom_denominator
    >= gibibyte_count(c.weight_gib) * serving_arm_headroom_numerator
}

// A CHECKPOINT LOADS WHEN THE RUNTIME REGISTERS ITS ARCHITECTURE, WHICH IS A MEMBERSHIP QUESTION.
// The field this replaced was `registers_glm5_next: Bool`, and the shape of that name is the whole
// defect: it answered ONE model's question, so every further model needed its own Bool, and a
// runtime row could not say anything at all about an architecture nobody had thought to ask about
// when the row was written. DeepSeek V4.1 is exactly that case -- `DeepseekV41ForCausalLM` did not
// exist when either runtime row was authored, and under the Bool both rows were silent about it
// while looking complete.
//
// THE SET IS A READING WE TOOK, NOT A FIELD THE IMAGE PUBLISHES, so the row carries the whole
// receipt -- host, date, instrument and answer -- rather than a bare list whose provenance would
// have to be restated in prose beside it. A miss is then a measured negative with a date on it
// rather than an absence of modelling, and the membership test itself is the receipt's own fold.
fn runtime_registers(rrow: ServingRuntimeRow, architecture: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  match rrow.registry_reading {
    Present { value: r } => registry_reading_contains(r: r, architecture: architecture)
    Absent => false
  }
}

// THE ROW'S DESIRED IMAGE AND ITS READING'S OBSERVED IMAGE ARE TWO FACTS, AND A REPIN SEPARATES THEM.
// The artifact's PIN is what this arm intends to run; the reading is
// what someone executed a registry call inside, frozen at the digest it was taken in. They agree
// today. The moment extdeps repins the image, they stop agreeing, and the old architecture list
// stops being evidence about the thing this arm would launch.
//
// AND THE PIN IS ONLY PART OF THE IDENTITY, which is what this branch added underneath the same check:
// a wire reference answers "which bytes does docker pull", while the artifact also carries the source
// revision and the patch set those bytes were built from. The currency question is still asked of the
// reference, because that is what a registry probe was executed against.
//
// SO THE ARM REFUSES RATHER THAN INHERITING. The alternative -- carrying the reading forward onto a
// digest nobody probed -- is the shape of every silent-wrongness failure this repository files: the
// row still looks measured, the provenance string still reads convincingly, and the fact underneath
// it has moved. A refusal here costs one probe; the other arm costs an engine that will not start
// and a receipt claiming it should.
// THIS CHECK IS NOT EQUALLY STRONG FOR EVERY ROW, AND THE WEAK CASE IS NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT TO
// BE DISCOVERED.
//
// It asks whether a row's registry reading was taken inside the image the row names, and it is a real
// join wherever the two sides come from independent places: gitcommit90 and sparkrun take their
// reference from an extdeps image module and their reading from a separate probe, so a drift between
// them reddens here.
//
// FOR THE NoPE-DERIVED ROW IT IS TAUTOLOGICAL, because that image has no extdeps authority -- it is a
// local build, not an upstream product -- so its digest has exactly one home, the registry reading
// itself, and the pin now reads it. Two independently editable spellings of one content id were the
// alternative, and a fork that a later equality check might catch is worse than a check that cannot
// fail: the fork is wrong the moment it drifts, the tautology is merely uninformative.
//
// WHAT WOULD MAKE IT INFORMATIVE AGAIN is an independent authority for that image's bytes -- the
// modelled build that produces it, which is not in this change. Until then this row's currency is
// asserted by construction and establishes nothing about the image.
fn runtime_reading_is_current(rrow: ServingRuntimeRow) -> Bool {
  match rrow.registry_reading {
    Present { value: r } => reading_applies_to(r: r, desired_reference: runtime_artifact_wire(a: rrow.artifact))
    Absent => false
  }
}

// TWO WAYS TO FAIL THE CURRENCY CHECK AND THEY OWE DIFFERENT WORK. A reading taken inside another digest
// means someone probed and then the image moved; no reading at all means nobody has looked. Both refuse,
// and a receipt that could not tell them apart would send a reader to re-probe an image that was never
// probed, or to hunt a repin that never happened -- so this fold names which one it hit.
fn serving_arm_admits(checkpoint: ServingCheckpoint, runtime: ServingRuntime, topology: ServingTopology) -> ServingArmVerdict {
  match serving_checkpoint_row(c: checkpoint) {
    Absent => ServingArmRefused { cause: "no checkpoint row: this checkpoint's repo, revision and footprint are unmodelled, so neither fit nor acquisition can be decided" as NonEmptyStr }
    Present { value: crow } =>
      match serving_runtime_row(r: runtime) {
        Absent => ServingArmRefused { cause: "no runtime row: this image's architecture registry has not been probed, so whether it can load the checkpoint is unknown" as NonEmptyStr }
        Present { value: rrow } =>
          if !runtime_reading_is_current(rrow: rrow) {
            match rrow.registry_reading {
              Absent => ServingArmRefused { cause: join([
                "no registry reading exists for ", runtime_artifact_wire(a: rrow.artifact) as String,
                ": nothing here has read this image's architecture registry inside its own digest, so whether it can load ",
                crow.architecture as String, " is unknown rather than false. One probe inside that digest settles it",
              ], "") as NonEmptyStr }
              Present { value: r } => ServingArmRefused { cause: join([
                "the runtime's architecture reading was taken inside ",
                r.image_reference as String,
                " and this arm now names ", runtime_artifact_wire(a: rrow.artifact) as String,
                ": the image was repinned without a new registry probe, so nothing here has looked inside the image this arm would launch",
              ], "") as NonEmptyStr }
            }
          } else if !runtime_registers(rrow: rrow, architecture: crow.architecture) {
            match rrow.registry_reading {
              Absent => ServingArmRefused { cause: "unreachable: an absent reading is never current, so this arm is guarded above" as NonEmptyStr }
              Present { value: r } => ServingArmRefused { cause: join([
                "the runtime does not register ", crow.architecture as String, " (vLLM ",
                rrow.vllm_version as String, " answered for ", to_string(length(r.architectures)),
                " architectures when its registry was read on ", r.observed_at as String,
                " and this is not one of them); no amount of weight transfer makes this tuple start",
              ], "") as NonEmptyStr }
            }
          } else if !serving_arm_fits(c: crow, t: topology) {
            ServingArmRefused { cause: join([
              "the checkpoint does not fit the topology: ", to_string(gibibyte_count(crow.weight_gib)),
              " GiB of weights against ", to_string(serving_topology_aggregate_pool_gib(t: topology)),
              " GiB aggregate unified pool across ", to_string(serving_topology_rank_count(t: topology)),
              " rank(s), before KV and activations",
            ], "") as NonEmptyStr }
          } else {
            ServingArmAdmitted
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── THE LAUNCH PROFILE IS A VALUE, NOT A SCATTER OF ROWS IN THE RENDERER ───────────────────────
//
// THE ARGV WAS ASSEMBLED FROM MODULE-LEVEL CONSTANTS IN gunbc.spark.serving_arm_launch, so the deployment
// being DECIDED and the deployment being LAUNCHED were two independently authored profiles that happened to
// describe the same engine. The decision named a Ray executor, 0.82, thirty-two sequences and an fp8 KV
// request; the renderer separately fixed mp, one sequence and an auto KV request. Nothing made a change to
// either follow the other, which is the authority break a review found: changing the selected runtime had no
// necessary effect on the image launched.
//
// SO THE PROFILE IS ONE VALUE THAT TRAVELS. A compatibility subject names it, a selection carries it, and the
// renderer reads argv out of it -- which makes "same subject" and "same argv" the same question instead of
// two that have to be kept in agreement by hand.
//
// IT CARRIES THE WIRE VALUES AND NOT THE POLICY THAT CHOSE THEM. The memory fraction here is the DECIDED one
// for the initial normal deployment; a fraction resolved per attempt from observed supply is intended to
// override it at plan time, and the admission reader that would own that resolution is a later change.
// Storing a resolved value in a profile would make the profile's identity change every time a machine's
// free memory did, which is why the override is a plan-time parameter rather than a row here.
// PREFIX CACHING IS A REQUEST THIS PROFILE MAKES, NOT A DEFAULT IT INHERITS. At the revision this fleet
// runs, an omitted flag is not answered by the engine's declared CacheConfig value: EngineArgs starts the
// field at None and resolves it from the MODEL configuration, so "say nothing and get True" was never the
// rule -- it was the rule for a checkpoint, and a different checkpoint would answer differently without
// anything here changing. extdeps.vllm.runtime_defaults now refuses to resolve an omitted request at that
// revision for exactly this reason.
//
// SO THE PROFILE NAMES A VALUE AND THE ARGV CARRIES IT. An explicit request needs no established upstream
// default, and it makes the launch's cache behaviour a decision recorded in the subject rather than a
// property of whatever the engine happened to infer.
//
// ADDING THIS FIELD CHANGES EVERY PROFILE IDENTITY, WHICH IS THE POINT. The identity is derived from the
// fields, and a compatibility refusal recorded against a profile that said nothing about prefix caching
// must not silently cover a launch that asks for it.
// kv_cache_dtype IS THE TYPED REQUEST, NOT A WIRE STRING. It stood as `kv_cache_dtype_wire:
// NonEmptyStr` until 2026-09-17, when the arm launched with `--kv-cache-dtype bf16` -- a spelling
// no vLLM build accepts -- and crash-looped 88 times on argument parse. A bare string can spell
// anything; the typed request can only spell what extdeps.vllm.engine_args admits, and the wire
// map there is the single authority corrected against the engine's own choice list.
type ServingLaunchProfile sole_constructor {
  max_model_len: TokenCount
  max_num_batched_tokens: TokenCount
  max_num_seqs: Nat
  decided_gpu_memory_basis_points: BasisPoint
  kv_cache_dtype: VllmKvCacheDtypeRequest
  serve_port: Port
  served_alias: NonEmptyStr
  prefix_caching: VllmPrefixCachingRequest
}

// THE PREFIX-CACHING REQUEST IS PART OF THE IDENTITY. Two launches differing only in whether they asked
// for prefix caching are two different launches, and a compatibility receipt recorded against one must not
// cover the other -- which is precisely what a key omitting this field would let it do.
// SPECULATION IS ABSENT FROM THIS KEY BECAUSE IT WAS ABSENT FROM THE LAUNCH.
//
// The profile carried `speculation_enabled: Bool` and this key spelled it `spec=on`/`spec=off`, so
// two subjects differing only in that flag were different subjects -- while arm_engine_argv rendered
// no speculative flag under either value. A profile set true planned an argv identical to false, so a
// compatibility receipt attached to that subject would have claimed a speculative configuration for
// an engine that never speculated: the borrowed-profile substitution this type exists to stop,
// committed by the type itself.
//
// A Bool COULD NOT HAVE BEEN REALIZED ANYWAY. vLLM's --speculative-config needs a method and a
// draft-token count; "true" names neither. When this arm wants speculation it arrives as the modelled
// policy that already exists -- extdeps.vllm.engine_args VllmSpeculativePolicy, and the argv shape
// gunbc.spark.glm_canary_converge renders from SpeculationPolicy -- carrying the basis a Bool drops.
fn serving_launch_profile_key(p: ServingLaunchProfile) -> String {
  join([
    "len=", to_string(token_count_value(t: p.max_model_len)),
    " batch=", to_string(token_count_value(t: p.max_num_batched_tokens)),
    " seqs=", to_string(p.max_num_seqs),
    " util_bp=", to_string(basis_point_count(bp: p.decided_gpu_memory_basis_points)),
    " kv=", vllm_kv_cache_dtype_wire(request: p.kv_cache_dtype) as String,
    " port=", to_string(p.serve_port),
    " alias=", p.served_alias as String,
    " prefix=", match p.prefix_caching {
      PrefixCachingOmitted => "omitted"
      PrefixCachingExplicit { enabled: e } => if e { "on" } else { "off" }
    },
  ], "")
}

// ── THE KV DTYPE IS ADMITTED PER ENGINE BUILD, NOT CHOSEN ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE LAW THAT GATEKEEPED THIS ROW IS PER-(KERNEL, BUILD), AND 2026-09-17 MEASURED BOTH BUILDS.
// The stock digest's kernel asserts pe_dim == 64 for fp8_ds_mla (cache_kernels.cu:928) and finds
// no attention backend at all under bfloat16, so THAT build serves this model on no spelling --
// both crash classes observed on the srv9 head 2026-09-17. The lane-built derived image (vLLM
// 8d09804 + flashinfer 0.7.0.dev20260910, gunbc.spark.vllm_runtime_image_build) inverts both
// readings: its only backend for this model is FLASHINFER_MLA_SPARSE_SM120, which supports ONLY
// the fp8 KV cache -- bfloat16 is refused by the selector with "kv_cache_dtype not supported"
// (smoke on srv10, 2026-09-17) -- and under fp8 it selects that backend, announces the fp8_ds_mla
// format, and proceeds (same smoke); its hand-built twin with the same recipe inputs served this
// model from 2026-09-13 to 2026-09-16, which on this build is possible only under an fp8 spelling.
// So the admission is a function of the BUILD first and the dtype second, and the build is a
// declared row rather than a fact re-derived at each call site.
//
// THE BUILD NAMES ITS IMAGE, and native_serving_realization derives the arm's image reference
// from the declared build -- one authority for "which engine the arm runs", so the admission and
// the pin cannot drift apart.
type GlmNativeEngineBuild
  = GlmNativeEngineStockG385dce36b
  | GlmNativeEngineDerived8d09804

fn glm_native_engine_build_image(build: GlmNativeEngineBuild) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match build {
    GlmNativeEngineStockG385dce36b =>
      "vllm/vllm-openai@sha256:819ec9c063412e5730d1b0e82046ba540d1bf991f3c4f661a849aae8a0c52374" as NonEmptyStr
    GlmNativeEngineDerived8d09804 =>
      "gunbc-vllm-glm53-gb10:derived-c99be081f219d123" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// THE ARM'S DECLARED BUILD: the lane-built derived image, receipt target/v41-runtime-image-build
// -receipt.txt 2026-09-17 (provenance=built-by-this-run, local_config_digest sha256:c7d63d15...).
// The stock digest is retained in the enum because it is the observed counter-example, not because
// it is a candidate: it serves this model on no admitted spelling.
data glm_native_engine_build: GlmNativeEngineBuild = GlmNativeEngineDerived8d09804

// RUNG HONESTY: this is mechanically preventable, not structural -- the profile is a data row and
// cannot refuse its own field, so the gate is the witness in
// dag/test/claim/spark/serving_arm_kv_dtype_witness_test.dag, which goes red if the profile
// carries a dtype its declared build refuses. The next-rung trigger is a profile constructor that
// takes the admission as an argument, making a refused dtype unconstructible.
type GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeAdmission
  = GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeAdmitted { dtype: VllmKvCacheDtypeRequest }
  | GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeRefused { dtype: VllmKvCacheDtypeRequest, cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn glm_native_kv_cache_dtype_admission(build: GlmNativeEngineBuild, dtype: VllmKvCacheDtypeRequest) -> GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeAdmission {
  match build {
    GlmNativeEngineDerived8d09804 =>
      match dtype {
        KvAuto => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeAdmitted { dtype: dtype }
        KvFp8 => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeAdmitted { dtype: dtype }
        KvFp8DsMla => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeAdmitted { dtype: dtype }
        KvBf16 => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeRefused {
          dtype: dtype,
          cause: "this build's only backend for the model (FLASHINFER_MLA_SPARSE_SM120) supports only the fp8 KV cache; the selector refused bfloat16 with 'kv_cache_dtype not supported' (smoke on srv10 2026-09-17)" as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        KvFp16 => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeRefused {
          dtype: dtype,
          cause: "unobserved, and this build's only backend for the model supports only the fp8 KV cache (the selector's refusal vocabulary for bfloat16, 2026-09-17)" as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      }
    GlmNativeEngineStockG385dce36b =>
      match dtype {
        KvBf16 => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeRefused {
          dtype: dtype,
          cause: "no valid attention backend for sparse MLA at pe_dim=0 with a non-fp8 KV cache on this build (srv9 head, 2026-09-17)" as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        KvFp16 => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeRefused {
          dtype: dtype,
          cause: "no observation admits float16 for this model on this build" as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        KvAuto => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeRefused {
          dtype: dtype,
          cause: "auto inherits this fp8-quantized checkpoint's dtype and resolves to fp8_ds_mla, whose kernel requires pe_dim == 64 on this build; GLM-5.3-Flash's pe_dim is 0 (cache_kernels.cu:928, observed crash under auto 2026-09-17)" as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        KvFp8 => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeRefused {
          dtype: dtype,
          cause: "resolves to fp8_ds_mla on this MLA checkpoint, whose kernel requires pe_dim == 64 on this build; GLM-5.3-Flash's pe_dim is 0 (cache_kernels.cu:928, observed 2026-09-17)" as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        KvFp8DsMla => GlmNativeKvCacheDtypeRefused {
          dtype: dtype,
          cause: "names the fp8_ds_mla kernel directly, which requires pe_dim == 64 on this build; GLM-5.3-Flash's pe_dim is 0 (cache_kernels.cu:928, observed 2026-09-17)" as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      }
  }
}

data glm_native_initial_profile: ServingLaunchProfile = ServingLaunchProfile {
  max_model_len: token_count(count: 262144),
  max_num_batched_tokens: token_count(count: 2048),
  max_num_seqs: 1,
  decided_gpu_memory_basis_points: basis_point(count: 8400),
  kv_cache_dtype: KvAuto,
  serve_port: 30000,
  served_alias: "glm-5.3-flash" as NonEmptyStr,
  prefix_caching: PrefixCachingExplicit { enabled: true },
}

// ── THE CONCURRENT PROFILE IS A DIFFERENT SUBJECT, AND THAT IS THE POINT ──────────────────────
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT AN EDIT TO THE ROW ABOVE. A ServingCompatibilitySubject is all five of its fields
// INCLUDING the profile, so changing a profile in place does not update a configuration -- it silently
// re-points every receipt attached to that subject at a configuration which never ran. This row exists
// because I did exactly that: I raised max_num_seqs from 1 to 16 inside the initial profile, which left
// gunbc.spark.serving_compatibility glm_native_on_derived_nope_standing asserting that a sixteen-sequence
// subject served on 2026-09-13, when what ran that day carried --max-num-seqs 1 and
// --gpu-memory-utilization 0.84. That is the borrowed-profile substitution the subject type exists to
// forbid, committed against the type by editing underneath it.
//
// THE TWO DELTAS FROM THE INITIAL PROFILE, both operator decisions of 2026-09-16:
// sixteen admitted sequences rather than one, on the operator's ruling that over-provisioning is the
// cheaper error while neither the runtime sequence capacity nor the KV-resident capacity is measured;
// and 8567 basis points rather than 8400, which is the engine's own advice -- it reports that 0.84 is
// effectively 0.8233 once CUDA-graph profiling is charged, and names 0.8567 as the value restoring the
// intended KV size.
//
// ITS STANDING IS NOT INHERITED FROM THE ROW ABOVE. This subject carries no execution receipt until an
// engine running THIS profile is observed serving, which is what
// gunbc.spark.serving_arm_launch intent_refusal will enforce: an unrun subject is admitted for a bounded
// experiment and refused for normal serving.
//
// THIS ROW'S max_num_seqs IS A CHOSEN BOUND, NOT A MEASURED ONE, and the row says so rather than dressing the
// number as a capacity reading. It stood at 1 under the launch policy "admit one until the runtime
// sequence capacity and the KV-resident capacity are both measured". On 2026-09-16 the operator
// replaced that posture ahead of any measurement: 16, on the stated basis that over-admission is
// preferable to under-admission while the measurement is unfunded ("better to have too many than
// too little"). The live evidence agrees the floor was far below the hardware: one sequence at a
// 262k window occupied 7% of the KV cache. This number is THIS arm's -- it may not travel to
// another model, quantisation or KV layout -- and the measurement that would replace a chosen
// bound with a derived one (concurrent-request KV occupancy) remains the standing trigger.
//
// AND ITS KV REQUEST IS KvFp8, NOT THE auto THE INITIAL ROW RECORDS, BECAUSE OF THE BUILD-KEYED
// ADMISSION ABOVE AND NOT A PREFERENCE. This is the profile the live arm launches from
// (gunbc.spark.native_serving_realization), and after the 2026-09-17 bf16 crash-loop it spells fp8.
// On the arm's declared build (the lane-built derived image) the only backend the selector admits
// for this model is FLASHINFER_MLA_SPARSE_SM120, which supports ONLY the fp8 KV cache -- bfloat16
// is refused by the engine itself (smoke, srv10 2026-09-17) -- and fp8 resolves to fp8_ds_mla,
// the format this build serves (its hand-built twin served 2026-09-13 to 2026-09-16). The pe_dim
// == 64 kernel law that forbids fp8 on the STOCK build is a per-(kernel, build) fact
// (extdeps.vllm.kv_cache) and does not bind here. The witness
// test.claim.spark.serving_arm_kv_dtype_witness goes red if this row carries a dtype the declared
// build refuses.
data glm_native_concurrent_profile: ServingLaunchProfile = ServingLaunchProfile {
  max_model_len: token_count(count: 262144),
  max_num_batched_tokens: token_count(count: 2048),
  max_num_seqs: 16,
  decided_gpu_memory_basis_points: basis_point(count: 8567),
  kv_cache_dtype: KvFp8,
  serve_port: glm_native_initial_profile.serve_port,
  served_alias: glm_native_initial_profile.served_alias,
  prefix_caching: PrefixCachingExplicit { enabled: true },
}

// THE EXL3 ARM'S PROFILE IS ITS OWN, and giving this subject the native one would make the receipt below
// assert something that was never measured. The canary is retired; this row exists so its EVIDENCE stays
// accurate, which is the only reason a retired route's facts are worth keeping at all.
// THE CANARY'S PROFILE IS READ OFF THE ENGINE THAT RAN, NOT ASSEMBLED FROM WHAT WAS DECIDED. A first
// revision of this row carried tp=4, 32 sequences and 2048 batched tokens -- the shared production decision
// parameters -- while the container on spark-0c75 was running tp=1, ONE sequence and 7168 batched tokens, as
// `docker inspect` reported on 2026-09-10. That is the borrowed-profile
// substitution this profile type exists to stop, committed inside the type itself.
//
// THE MEMORY FRACTION IS THE ENGINE'S DEFAULT, BECAUSE THE LINE NAMES NONE -- verified against the
// container: glm-canary on spark-0c75 carries no --gpu-memory-utilization in its argv. Recording a
// decided 0.82 here would claim an argument the launch never sent.
//
// AND THE FIGURE IS NOW READ FROM THE DEFAULT RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBED. This row said 9000, which is
// neither the decision parameters' 0.82 nor the engine's default: at Vllm487ecf187
// defaults_gpu_utilization answers 9200. So the annotation was right about the PRINCIPLE and the
// value beneath it was a third number nobody sent -- the row warned against claiming an unsent
// argument and then claimed a different one. Deriving it means the profile cannot drift from the
// revision whose default it is describing.
data glm_exl3_canary_profile: ServingLaunchProfile = ServingLaunchProfile {
  max_model_len: token_count(count: 262144),
  max_num_batched_tokens: token_count(count: 7168),
  max_num_seqs: 1,
  decided_gpu_memory_basis_points: defaults_gpu_utilization(r: Vllm487ecf187),
  kv_cache_dtype: KvFp8,
  serve_port: 30001,
  served_alias: "glm-5.3-flash-exl3-canary" as NonEmptyStr,
  prefix_caching: PrefixCachingOmitted,
}


// ── THE RUNTIME THIS FLEET BUILT FOR THE NATIVE CHECKPOINT ────────────────────────────────────
//
// IT IS ITS OWN RUNTIME BECAUSE IT IS DIFFERENT BYTES, and the two prior native attempts are exactly why
// that matters: the official SM121 image refused at the kernel shape, and the community EXL3 image could
// not map the native checkpoint's tensor names. Both refusals are recorded against THEIR subjects, and
// the row that records them says a different backend, layout or patched image is a different subject and
// must be MEASURED rather than inferred. This is that different image.
//
// PINNED BY ITS CONTENT AND NOT BY ITS TAG, which took a measurement to get right. The image was never
// pushed, so `RepoDigests` is empty and a `repo@sha256:` reference does not resolve -- measured on srv9
// 2026-09-13. The bare content id DOES resolve, both for inspect and for creating a container, so it is
// what the pin names. Worth recording beside it: the same daemon refuses that id as a Dockerfile FROM,
// which is a different resolver in the same engine.
//
// THE PATCH SET IS THE THREE WHEELS, which is what `patch_set` exists for -- an image taken as published
// carries an empty list, and one that installed something must say what. Naming them here is what keeps
// this row distinguishable from an official build at the same vLLM revision.
data glm53_nope_derived_vllm_version: NonEmptyStr = "0.1.dev1+g8d09804c8" as NonEmptyStr

data glm53_nope_derived_row: ServingRuntimeRow = ServingRuntimeRow {
  runtime: GunbcGlm53NopeDerivedGb10,
  artifact: runtime_artifact_identity(
    pin: PinnedByDigest { reference: glm53_nope_derived_registry_reading.image_reference },
    source_revision: (defaults_source_revision(r: Vllm8d09804)).full_revision,
    patch_set: [
      "flashinfer_python-0.7.0.dev20260910-py3-none-any.whl" as NonEmptyStr,
      "flashinfer_cubin-0.7.0.dev20260910-py3-none-any.whl" as NonEmptyStr,
      "flashinfer_jit_cache-0.7.0.dev20260910+cu130-cp39-abi3-manylinux_2_28_aarch64.whl" as NonEmptyStr,
    ],
    provenance: "built by gunbc.spark.vllm_runtime_image_build on srv9 2026-09-13 as a derived layer over the GLM NoPE base sha256:8af305a7950f...5eec7, installing the three FlashInfer 0.7.0.dev20260910 wheels with --no-deps --force-reinstall. The base relationship is not asserted from the FROM line, which can only name a tag: the derived image's RootFS layers were read after the build and the base's layers are a prefix of them. The base itself remains AdoptedFromOutOfBandBuild -- this row does not launder that standing, it records a layer built on top of it" as NonEmptyStr,
  ),
  hub_cli_path: "/usr/local/bin/hf" as NonEmptyStr,
  vllm_version: glm53_nope_derived_vllm_version,
  registry_reading: Present { value: glm53_nope_derived_registry_reading },
  reasoning_projection: Present { value: ParsedReasoningField { field: "reasoning" as NonEmptyStr } },
  provenance: "read inside this exact image on srv9 2026-09-13: 378 registered architectures including all three Glm5Next entries and no DeepseekV41ForCausalLM; flashinfer 0.7.0.dev20260910 and vllm 0.1.dev1+g8d09804c8 reported by import; entrypoint [vllm, serve]; platform linux/arm64; hf at /usr/local/bin/hf; the reasoning parser name this fleet's argv requests (glm45) and the tool parser name (glm47) both RESOLVE here, to Glm47MoeParserReasoningAdapter and Glm47MoeModelToolParser -- established by lookup rather than by reading the registry dict, which is populated lazily and answers empty to a bare import. The NoPE kernel probes both pass: the writer accepts pe_dim 0 and zeroes the reserved tail, and the SM120 sparse reader accepts the 512+0 layout when asked with arbitrary_fp32 scales. THE REASONING PROJECTION IS NO LONGER ABSENT, and it was filled the only way it could be: this image served. A routed completion against the four-rank arm returned a response carrying a `reasoning` member DISTINCT from `content`, with 47 of its 73 completion tokens counted as reasoning_tokens -- so the parser that resolves here also SEPARATES, which resolution alone never showed. That a name resolves in a registry and that a served response comes back split are two different facts, and only the second can be read from a launch" as NonEmptyStr,
  claimed_defaults_revision: Present { value: Vllm8d09804 },
}
